  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cool air mass in place will likely brings frost to \nparts of the region tonight, particularly across the Poconos and \nnorthwestern New Jersey.\n\nCanadian high pressure has brought a cool airmass to the area. The\nhigh will crest over the area through this evening before \ngradually beginning to slide offshore later tonight. This setup \nwill lead to very good radiational cooling conditions tonight \ndue to the mainly clear sky and diminishing winds under the \ncenter of the high. For these reasons our forecast continues to \nundercut NBM temps for most areas with the mention of patchy \nfrost in the normally cooler areas including interior southern \nNJ. Farther north, frost is likely to be a little more extensive\nso we continue with the Frost Advisory for tonight for our \nPocono zones and Sussex County NJ. These area have the best \nchances for temperatures radiating to near freezing. That said, \nwouldn't be shocked if some colder locations farther south, \nparticularly across the NJ Pine Barrens, made a run at 32 \ntonight. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tranquil weather will prevail this week continuing \ninto the weekend with no significant weather hazards anticipated. \n\nHigh pressure will be in control through most of the week resulting \nin mostly clear/sunny skies. Following tonight's chilly weather \ntemperatures will moderate Wednesday through Thursday as the flow \nturns southwesterly ahead of the next cold front. In fact highs by \nThursday afternoon look to reach the middle to upper 70s for most of \nthe area. The cold front moves through by late Thursday as a dry \nfront and will be followed by high pressure re-establishing itself \nover the area for Friday through at least Saturday with seasonable \ntemperatures. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...The next system will approach the area later this \nweekend bringing chances for rain as early as Sunday afternoon.\n\nHigh pressure shifts offshore Sunday as the next low pressure system \napproaches from the south and west. There are some model differences \nregarding this system and its arrival time in the area. The ECMWF \nhas the rain shield from the system arriving in the areas beginning \nSunday afternoon while most of our other guidance holds the system \noff until Sunday night into Monday. In any event, the system should \nbe fairly progressive but will likely have significant moisture with \nit (PWATs potentially reaching around 2 inches) so the rain could be \nheavy at times with the potential for some areas to see 1-2 inches.",
